--- a/packages/api/session-controller/README.md
+++ b/packages/api/session-controller/README.md
@@ -83,6 +83,9 @@ The controller composes `ArchivedSessionGate` through `ctx.plugin`: it loads onc
 <a id="client-references"></a>
 ## Client references
 
+The Client controller composes registered mapped sources through `ctx.sessions.registerSource?.(owner, source)`. A source provides an independent `ISessions` adapter, stable ownership predicates for Session and Workspace identities, and a unique key for the facade lifetime. The adapter must translate all identity-bearing metadata and preserve its own binding Context; the facade never constructs remote bindings through the local SessionManager. Catalogs compose into the existing list. Identity-addressed commands route to their owning controller; mixed-source create reuse and subagent addresses are refused. Withdrawal removes rows, releases facade-owned references, and reserves the withdrawn identity family so stale navigation cannot call the local Host. Search remains local; root permissions, attachments, model catalogs and source-selection UI require their own integration.
+
+
 `sessions.retain(target, { source, signal? })` immediately acquires one exact Client generation and starts its shared initial history opening. The target is a known Session id or a durable direct-parent subagent address; the Host validates an explicit address when history opens. The returned reference supports idempotent `release()` and `Symbol.dispose`; its `ready` Promise follows the shared `Session.open()` result and resolves to the exact binding when that attempt settles, including when a Remote failure is represented by `openState: 'error'`. It rejects when `Session.open()` rejects, its waiter is cancelled, or the reference is released early. Cancelling one waiter does not cancel another owner's opening. `sessions.using(target, options, operation)` waits for that settlement, holds its reference until the callback settles, and propagates rejected readiness and callback failures.
 
 References keep local Session data, scoped Contexts, and history streams alive, not Host Agents. Ordinary Session retention never adds a catalog row. Retained subagents with a known direct-parent address keep a fallback row and notify list readers even before the parent catalog arrives; these rows do not join Host-list membership. Fork-title assignment sends rename directly and applies the returned title projection without retaining the child or opening its history. Final release withdraws the generation before teardown; later acquisition can create a new generation with the same id. `binding(id)` and `scope(id)` only borrow an existing generation. `retainInfo(id)` observes stable read-only source counts independently of catalog membership and performs no history I/O. Consumer source keys are declaration-merge extensible; navigation and completion acknowledgement belong to UI consumers, not this Controller. See [Client Session references](../../../.agents/notes/implemented/architecture/2026-09-15-client-session-references.md) for ownership and teardown rules. An unknown-mode subagent address permits history reads with direct-parent consistency validation; access is checked separately on the child Session. Successful descriptor restoration determines the displayed mode; failures remain local to the child. Control requests carry only the target id, and the Host checks continuation availability. Client snapshots retain only the subagent address; runtime admission owns continuation availability.
